WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.024
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.024 --> 00:00:05.690
ROBERT DESIMONE: MIT has some of
the world's greatest scientists

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.020
working on autism research.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.170
And this new center
was established

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:12.650
to fund all new research
direction by those scientists.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.710
We're going to apply
revolutionary new approaches

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.860
in biology to
understanding autism.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.350
OMAR ABUDAYYEH: So gene
therapy is a type of treatment

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.120
that aims to correct
the problems that

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.530
occur in your DNA code.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:25.660
My name is Omar Abudayyeh.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.280
I'm an MD PhD student
at MIT and Harvard.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:32.930
And Jonathan Gootenberg
is my work spouse.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.600
One of the technologies
we've really been developing

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.724
is this new RNA editing
technology called REPAIR.

00:00:37.724 --> 00:00:39.140
JONATHAN GOOTENBERG:
And REPAIR is

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.680
a system for taking
a specific letter

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.677
and changing it to
a different letter.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.760
And so one really exciting
thing you could imagine

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.404
is, using our new
technology, we could

00:00:48.404 --> 00:00:50.570
screen the hundreds or
thousands of mutations who've

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:54.021
seen patients with autism in
a dish or some sort of model

00:00:54.021 --> 00:00:55.520
and actually try
to understand, what

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.590
are the most relevant mutations,
what pathways they act through.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.590
And then when you get
that better understanding,

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.421
you start to have a map for
building better treatments.

00:01:03.421 --> 00:01:04.879
GLORIA CHOI: So we
are specifically

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:08.300
looking at how the immune
activation during pregnancy

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.410
can lead to brain disorders
such as autism in offspring.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:15.490
We would like to
understand how the gut

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.520
microbiome in the
mother's body actually can

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.960
modulate this entire process.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.320
And we want to ask whether
we can take advantage

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.370
of this process to
either prevent or treat

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.720
the development of autism.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.780
TOBIAS KAISER: Hey,
I'm Tobias Kaiser.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.490
I work in Guoping Feng's lab.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.950
And I'm also the
Tan Yang fellow.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.350
My goals are similar to
those of most research.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.375
You want to change
the world in some way.

00:01:41.375 --> 00:01:43.260
So I'm doing two things mainly.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.580
One is I understand
a gene that's highly

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.380
implicated in autism better.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.460
And the second is that we
try to develop gene therapies

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:53.430
for those disorders.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.096
So what I'm really
hoping is that, as we

00:01:55.096 --> 00:01:58.070
focus on one disorder,
we can actually

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.650
expand these and generalize
some of these strategies,

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.720
also, to other disorders.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.920
A.J. HASKINS: So this is a
really interesting puzzle

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.419
I'm A.J. Haskins.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:12.470
I work in this building for
labs that recruit individuals

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:13.460
with autism.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.970
So Dima and I have worked
on a couple of projects

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.340
together recruiting
individuals with autism

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.710
to do brain imaging studies.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.400
DIMA AYYASH: It's really
hard to recruit and bring

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.755
in participants into
the lab for a study.

00:02:26.755 --> 00:02:29.570
And so we often end
up with small samples.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.580
And the sample is
not representative

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:33.050
of the whole spectrum.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:37.040
What we are trying to
do is to aggregate data

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.760
from various labs at MIT,
but eventually, various

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.040
labs from all over
the world, ultimately,

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.487
to address a few of the
challenges of autism research.

00:02:48.487 --> 00:02:50.570
ROBERT DESIMONE: When you
put the very best people

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.440
onto a problem and you
give them all the resources

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:59.420
they need to solve that problem,
I can't help but be hopeful.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.300
I think we're entering a
new era of autism research.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.430
And the end point is
going to be new therapies.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:07.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

